{
  "dom_id": "step-q352401",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)-1.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(3t),$ determine $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(3t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t))=6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(3t))}{6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-(3{sec}^{2}\u2061(3t)))}{6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}\u2061(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}\u2061(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{9})=-4.\n$$",
